VectorClust: Mathematical Background, Proofs, and Algorithms

Author: ouadi Maakoul

Abstract

Vector similarity search on heterogeneous hardware clusters is constrained by memory capacities, latency objectives, and power budgets that traditional algorithms largely ignore. This thesis presents a theoretical framework for capacity-constrained vector search, formalizing it as a capacitated k-clustering problem with a general objective: minimizing a monotone symmetric norm of the per-vector distances. This class captures k-center, k-median, and tail-quantile objectives as special cases.

We adapt the fixed-parameter tractable (FPT) approximation framework of Dai, Li & Peng (ICALP 2026) for capacitated minimum-norm clustering to the vector-search domain, and we extend it to outliers, (f,g)-clustering, coresets, and a hierarchical federated routing scheme. The resulting algorithm, VectorClust, runs in time (k\log n/\varepsilon)^{O(k)}\cdot\operatorname{poly}(n) and achieves a (3+O(\varepsilon))-approximation for any monotone symmetric norm. This factor is optimal under \mathrm{FPT}\neq W[1] for the L_\infty special case (k-center). For the L_1 special case (capacitated k-median), the tight FPT factor is unknown: VectorClust matches the best known upper bound 3+O(\varepsilon), while the best known lower bound is 1+2/e-\varepsilon. We state this asymmetry explicitly and do not claim optimality for L_1.

Two extensions—multi-resource capacities and weighted query norms—are presented as open problems rather than established results, because neither is supported by the current literature. All results are purely mathematical; no experiments, physical tests, or code training were performed, and empirical validation is left for future work.

