Introduction

Motivation

Vector similarity search has become a foundational primitive in modern AI systems, from retrieval-augmented generation to recommendation and dense retrieval. As these systems scale, they are deployed on heterogeneous hardware clusters: GPUs with varying memory capacities, compute capabilities, and interconnect bandwidths. Traditional vector search algorithms treat the problem as pure similarity optimization, ignoring critical deployment constraints:

· Hardware capacity limits. Each GPU has a finite memory budget and cannot store an unbounded number of vectors.
· Query latency objectives. Both worst-case (tail) and average latency must be controlled, often simultaneously.
· Economic and power constraints. Heterogeneous hardware mixes, energy consumption, and cost efficiency matter.

These constraints turn vector search into a capacity-constrained optimization problem. In this work, we formalize vector search under capacity limits as a capacitated k-clustering problem, where each GPU is a facility with a capacity, vectors are clients, and the distance metric captures embedding similarity together with computational load. We then design an algorithm, VectorClust, that leverages recent breakthroughs in fixed-parameter tractable (FPT) approximation algorithms for clustering to provide provable guarantees on the latency distribution under arbitrary monotone symmetric norms.

Contributions

This thesis makes the following contributions:

1. A unified formulation of capacity-constrained vector search as capacitated k-clustering under monotone symmetric norms. This formulation encompasses standard objectives (k-center, k-median, top-\ell latency) and allows simultaneous optimization of multiple latency metrics.
2. An FPT approximation algorithm (VectorClust) that, for any fixed number of partitions k, runs in time (k\log n/\varepsilon)^{O(k)}\cdot\operatorname{poly}(n) and achieves a (3+O(\varepsilon))-approximation for any monotone symmetric norm. The core algorithmic framework is inherited from Dai, Li & Peng (ICALP 2026); the application to vector search and the extensions below are adaptations.
3. Corrected optimality statement. The factor 3+O(\varepsilon) is optimal under \mathrm{FPT}\neq W[1] for the L_\infty special case (k-center). For the L_1 special case (capacitated k-median), the tight FPT factor is unknown: the best known upper bound is 3+\varepsilon (Cohen-Addad & Li, ICALP 2019; recovered by Dai et al.), and the best known lower bound is 1+2/e-\varepsilon inherited from the uncapacitated case. We claim neither optimality nor non-optimality for L_1.
4. Outlier extension. We adapt the ring-sampling technique of Dabas, Gupta & Inamdar (TCS 2025) to preserve the (3+O(\varepsilon))-approximation with an outlier budget m. The dummy-facility device appears only inside their proofs; the reduction itself is ring-sampling based.
5. (f,g)-clustering extension. We extend the LP and guessing phase to handle separate intra- and inter-cluster monotone symmetric norms, following Herold, Kipouridis & Spoerhase (SODA 2025). We note that completing this extension would improve the polynomial-time state of the art, since even uncapacitated general (f,g)-clustering has only an O(k)-approximation.
6. Coreset reduction. We state a conditional coreset existence claim of size \operatorname{poly}(k,\varepsilon^{-1},\log n), building on the sensitivity-sampling framework of Braverman, Feldman & Lang (ACML 2021) and the unconditional capacitated k-median coreset of Cohen-Addad & Li.
7. Federated routing. We give an O(\log k) query-time hierarchical routing scheme, with an approximation degradation stated under explicit diameter assumptions.

Scope and Limitations

This thesis is purely theoretical. We do not implement VectorClust, we do not run experiments, and we do not train or evaluate any model. All claims are mathematical. Where a proof is incomplete or an extension is unsupported by the literature, we state this explicitly.

Two extensions require particular caution:

· Multi-resource capacities. Even the two-dimensional case inherits bin-packing hardness. No FPT result is known. We present this as an open problem.
· Weighted query norms. The original “weighted top-\ell norm” is not symmetric under coordinate permutations and therefore falls outside the monotone symmetric norm class. We present two viable reformulations and do not claim a (3+O(\varepsilon))-approximation for a weighted symmetric norm class.

Related Work

Capacity-constrained clustering. Polynomial-time constant-factor approximations exist for capacitated k-center (factor 3, Khuller & Sussmann 2000) and capacitated k-median (factor 1+2/e, Li 2022). These are not FPT. The recent breakthrough by Dai, Li & Peng (ICALP 2026) gave tight FPT approximations for both problems, parameterized by k, and extended the result to any monotone symmetric norm. Their work forms the theoretical foundation for VectorClust.

(f,g)-clustering. Introduced by Herold, Kipouridis & Spoerhase (SODA 2025), this framework subsumes k-center, k-median, Min-Sum of Radii, and Min-Load k-Clustering. Their polynomial-time approximations range from O(\log^2 n) to O(k); the FPT adaptation is new.

Outliers. Dabas, Gupta & Inamdar (TCS 2025) give the first constant-factor FPT approximation for capacitated k-median with outliers, achieving (3+\varepsilon) in time f(k,m,\varepsilon)\cdot|I_m|^{O(1)} via ring sampling. Our outlier extension adapts this technique to general monotone symmetric norms.

Coresets. Cohen-Addad & Li (ICALP 2019) give an unconditional coreset for capacitated k-median of size O(k^2\log^2 n/\varepsilon^3). The sensitivity-sampling framework of Braverman, Feldman & Lang (ACML 2021) provides a general recipe. Extending coresets to general monotone symmetric norms under capacity constraints remains open.

Systems literature. Learning-augmented indexing (FAISS, HNSW) and SSD-based search (DiskANN) provide heuristic solutions without provable guarantees. These are orthogonal to our theoretical contribution.

