Chapter 1: Introduction

1.1 Problem Statement

We are given a set of vectors V=\{v_1,\dots,v_n\}\subset\mathbb{R}^d. These vectors represent data points to be partitioned among k facilities (GPUs). Each facility i has a capacity u_i\in\mathbb{N}, indicating the maximum number of vectors it can host. The capacity may be derived from memory limits, compute budgets, or a combination.

A distance function d:V\times V\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge0} is defined, satisfying the triangle inequality, symmetry, and non-negativity. In vector search, d often measures dissimilarity between embeddings; we can also incorporate load and hardware characteristics as additive components, provided the resulting function remains a metric.

The goal is to assign each vector to a facility, respecting capacities, and to minimize an objective that depends on the distances from each vector to its assigned facility's representative. Let \mu_i be the representative of facility i. An assignment \sigma:V\to[k] yields distances d(v_j,\mu_{\sigma(v_j)}). We denote the vector of these distances by \mathbf{d}=(d(v_1,\mu_{\sigma(v_1)}),\dots,d(v_n,\mu_{\sigma(v_n)})).

Definition 1.1 (Capacitated k-clustering). Given V, a set of facilities F, capacities \{u_i\}_{i\in F}, and a monotone symmetric norm f, find an assignment \sigma:V\to F with |\sigma^{-1}(i)|\le u_i that minimizes f(\mathbf{d}).

1.2 Special Cases

Several well-known clustering problems are captured by choosing f appropriately:

· k-center. f=L_\infty, i.e., f(\mathbf{d})=\max_j d_j. This minimizes worst-case latency.
· k-median. f=L_1, i.e., f(\mathbf{d})=\sum_j d_j. This minimizes average latency.
· Top-\ell norm. For 0<\ell\le n,
  \|\mathbf{d}\|_{\text{top-}\ell}=\max_{S\subseteq[n],|S|=\lfloor\ell\rfloor}\sum_{j\in S}d_j+(\ell-\lfloor\ell\rfloor)d_{(\lceil\ell\rceil)},
  where d_{(1)}\ge\dots\ge d_{(n)} are sorted. This focuses on the worst \ell distances (tail latency).

More generally, any monotone symmetric norm can be expressed as a convex combination of top-\ell norms (Lemma 2.1 in Chapter 2).

1.3 Outliers

In many practical scenarios, some vectors may be considered “noise” and need not be assigned to any facility, or they may be assigned to a special outlier facility with no capacity limit but incurring a penalty. We extend Definition 1.1 to allow up to m outliers.

Definition 1.2 (Capacitated k-clustering with outliers). Given an additional parameter m, find an assignment \sigma:V\to F\cup\{\bot\} with |\sigma^{-1}(i)|\le u_i for all i\in F and |\sigma^{-1}(\bot)|\le m, minimizing f(\mathbf{d}) over the assigned points. The distance for outliers is defined as 0.

The dummy-facility reduction is valid for any monotone symmetric norm: since f is componentwise non-decreasing, reducing any component of \mathbf{d} to 0 yields a vector \mathbf{d}' with f(\mathbf{d}')\le f(\mathbf{d}). The ring-sampling technique of Dabas, Gupta & Inamdar (TCS 2025) achieves a (3+\varepsilon)-approximation for capacitated k-median with outliers in FPT time.

1.4 Multi-Resource Capacities (Open)

Modern hardware often has multiple resource constraints: memory, compute, and I/O bandwidth. We model this as a multi-dimensional capacity vector.

Definition 1.3 (Multi-resource capacitated clustering). Each facility i has a capacity vector \mathbf{u}_i\in\mathbb{N}^R. Each client j consumes \mathbf{w}_j\in\mathbb{N}^R. Feasibility requires \sum_{j\in\sigma^{-1}(i)}\mathbf{w}_j\le\mathbf{u}_i coordinatewise.

Hardness note. The multi-resource variant is at least as hard as multi-dimensional bin packing, which is strongly NP-hard even for fixed R. Under ETH, any approximation algorithm for non-uniform capacitated clustering requires super-FPT running time. An FPT result must parameterize by R in addition to k, or impose structure on the capacity vectors. We present this as an open problem (Open Problem 5).

