Chapter 2: Problem Formulation and Mathematical Background

2.1 Capacitated Clustering for Vector Search

We are given a set of vectors V=\{v_1,\dots,v_n\}\subset\mathbb{R}^d, representing data points to be partitioned among k facilities (GPUs). Each facility i has a capacity u_i\in\mathbb{N}, indicating the maximum number of vectors it can host. The capacity may be derived from memory limits, compute budgets, or a combination.

A distance function d:V\times V\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge0} is defined, satisfying the triangle inequality, symmetry, and non-negativity. In vector search, d often measures dissimilarity between embeddings; we can also incorporate load and hardware characteristics as additive components, provided the resulting function remains a metric.

The goal is to assign each vector to a facility, respecting capacities, and to minimize an objective that depends on the distances from each vector to its assigned facility's representative. Let \mu_i be the representative of facility i. An assignment \sigma:V\to[k] yields distances d(v_j,\mu_{\sigma(v_j)}). We denote the vector of these distances by \mathbf{d}=(d(v_1,\mu_{\sigma(v_1)}),\dots,d(v_n,\mu_{\sigma(v_n)})).

Definition 2.1 (Capacitated k-clustering). Given a set V of clients, a set F of potential facility locations (which may be the same as V), a capacity u_i for each i\in F, and a monotone symmetric norm f, find an assignment \sigma:V\to F with |\sigma^{-1}(i)|\le u_i that minimizes f(\mathbf{d}), where \mathbf{d}_j=d(v_j,\mu_{\sigma(v_j)}).

In our context, the set of facilities is the set of GPUs themselves; we can assume each GPU corresponds to a point in the metric space (e.g., its identifier). For simplicity, we identify each facility with its index i.

2.2 Special Cases

Several well-known clustering problems are captured by choosing f appropriately:

· k-center. f=L_\infty, i.e., f(\mathbf{d})=\max_j d_j. This minimizes the maximum distance (worst-case latency).
· k-median. f=L_1, i.e., f(\mathbf{d})=\sum_j d_j. This minimizes the total (or average) distance.
· Top-\ell norm. For 0<\ell\le n,
  \|\mathbf{d}\|_{\text{top-}\ell}=\max_{S\subseteq[n],|S|=\lfloor\ell\rfloor}\sum_{j\in S}d_j+(\ell-\lfloor\ell\rfloor)d_{(\lceil\ell\rceil)},
  where d_{(1)}\ge\dots\ge d_{(n)} are sorted. This focuses on the worst \ell distances (e.g., tail latency).

More generally, any monotone symmetric norm can be expressed as a convex combination of top-\ell norms (Lemma 2.2).

2.3 Outliers

In many practical scenarios, some vectors may be considered “noise” and need not be assigned to any facility, or they may be assigned to a special outlier facility with no capacity limit but incurring a penalty. We extend Definition 2.1 to allow up to m outliers.

Definition 2.2 (Capacitated k-clustering with outliers). Given an additional parameter m (outlier budget), find an assignment \sigma:V\to F\cup\{\bot\} with |\sigma^{-1}(i)|\le u_i for all i\in F and |\sigma^{-1}(\bot)|\le m (where \bot denotes the outlier class), minimizing f(\mathbf{d}) over the assigned points (the distance for outliers is defined as 0).

The reduction is valid for any monotone symmetric norm: since f is componentwise non-decreasing, reducing any component of \mathbf{d} to 0 yields a vector \mathbf{d}' with f(\mathbf{d}')\le f(\mathbf{d}). Dabas, Gupta & Inamdar (TCS 2025) establish a (3+\varepsilon)-approximation for capacitated k-median with outliers via ring sampling, FPT in k,m,\varepsilon. Our extension adapts this to general monotone symmetric norms.

2.4 Multi-Resource Capacities (Open Problem)

Modern hardware often has multiple resource constraints: memory (number of vectors), compute (e.g., FLOPs), and I/O bandwidth. We model this as a multi-dimensional capacity vector.

Definition 2.3 (Multi-resource capacitated clustering). Each facility i has a capacity vector \mathbf{u}_i=(u_i^1,\dots,u_i^R)\in\mathbb{N}^R. Each client j consumes a resource consumption vector \mathbf{w}_j\in\mathbb{N}^R. The assignment must satisfy \sum_{j\in\sigma^{-1}(i)}\mathbf{w}_j\le\mathbf{u}_i coordinatewise.

Hardness note. The multi-resource variant is at least as hard as multi-dimensional bin packing, which is strongly NP-hard even for fixed R. Under ETH, any approximation algorithm for non-uniform capacitated clustering requires super-FPT running time. An FPT result must parameterize by R in addition to k, or impose structure on the capacity vectors. We present this as an open problem.

2.5 Monotone Symmetric Norms

Let \mathbb{R}^n_{\ge0} be the set of non-negative vectors. A function f:\mathbb{R}^n_{\ge0}\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge0} is a monotone symmetric norm if it satisfies:

1. Positivity. f(\mathbf{x})=0\iff\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{0}.
2. Homogeneity. f(\lambda\mathbf{x})=\lambda f(\mathbf{x}) for all \lambda\ge0.
3. Triangle inequality. f(\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{y})\le f(\mathbf{x})+f(\mathbf{y}).
4. Monotonicity. If \mathbf{x}\le\mathbf{y} coordinatewise, then f(\mathbf{x})\le f(\mathbf{y}).
5. Symmetry. f(\mathbf{x})=f(\pi(\mathbf{x})) for any permutation \pi.

Lemma 2.1 (Representation via top-\ell norms). For any monotone symmetric norm f and any \mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^n_{\ge0}, there exist weights w_1\ge w_2\ge\dots\ge w_n\ge0 such that

f(\mathbf{x})=\sum_{\ell=1}^n(w_\ell-w_{\ell+1})\|\mathbf{x}\|_{\text{top-}\ell},

where w_{n+1}:=0. Conversely, any convex combination of top-\ell norms yields a monotone symmetric norm.

Proof. This is a standard fact from the theory of symmetric norms. The set of top-\ell norms forms the set of extreme rays of the cone of monotone symmetric norms. A full proof appears in Chakrabarty & Swamy (2019). The same representation is used by Herold, Kipouridis & Spoerhase (SODA 2025) and Dai, Li & Peng (ICALP 2026). \square

Thus, approximating f reduces to approximating all top-\ell norms simultaneously.

Definition 2.4 (Top-\ell norm). For \ell\in[0,n],

\|\mathbf{x}\|_{\text{top-}\ell}=\min_{t\ge0}\left(\sum_{j=1}^n(x_j-t)_++\ell t\right),

where (x)_+=\max\{0,x\}. The optimum is achieved when t equals the \ell-th largest coordinate.

2.6 Fixed-Parameter Tractability (FPT)

An algorithm is fixed-parameter tractable with parameter k if its running time is bounded by g(k)\cdot\operatorname{poly}(n), where g is some computable function depending only on k. For clustering, k is the number of clusters (facilities). In vector search, k is typically small (e.g., number of GPUs, often \le100), making FPT algorithms practical even if the exponential dependence on k is large.

2.7 Hardness of Capacitated Clustering

Theorem 2.2 (Hardness results).

· Capacitated k-center and k-median are NP-hard for all capacities u_i\ge2.
· Under the assumption \mathrm{FPT}\neq W[1], there is no FPT algorithm achieving approximation factor 3-\varepsilon for capacitated k-center, for any \varepsilon>0.
· For capacitated k-median, the best known FPT upper bound is 3+\varepsilon (Cohen-Addad & Li, ICALP 2019; recovered by Dai et al.). The best known lower bound is 1+2/e-\varepsilon, inherited from the uncapacitated case under Gap-ETH. The tight FPT factor for capacitated k-median lies in [1+2/e,3] and is open.

These results are established in Cohen-Addad & Li (ICALP 2019) and Dai, Li & Peng (ICALP 2026). They show that our approximation ratio is optimal for FPT algorithms in the L_\infty case, but not known to be optimal for the L_1 case.

2.8 Coresets and Sensitivity Sampling

Definition 2.5 (Coreset). Let P be a point set and \mathcal{Q} a family of queries. A weighted subset (S,w) with S\subseteq P is an \varepsilon-coreset if for every q\in\mathcal{Q},

\left|\sum_{s\in S}w(s)f(s,q)-\sum_{p\in P}f(p,q)\right|\le\varepsilon\sum_{p\in P}f(p,q).

Theorem 2.3 (Sensitivity sampling; Braverman, Feldman & Lang, ACML 2021). If the total sensitivity of a point set under a cost function is t, then sampling O(t\log t/\varepsilon^2) points with probability proportional to sensitivity yields an \varepsilon-coreset with constant probability.

Theorem 2.4 (Capacitated k-median coreset; Cohen-Addad & Li, ICALP 2019). There is a coreset of size O(k^2\log^2 n/\varepsilon^3) for capacitated k-median in general metric spaces. This construction is unconditional.

Open problem. Coresets for general monotone symmetric norms under capacity constraints are not established. The reduction via Lemma 2.1 does not immediately preserve sensitivity bounds under capacity constraints; each top-\ell component may require separate sampling, and the union bound over n components can blow up the coreset size unless a tighter argument is found.

2.9 (f,g)-Clustering

Definition 2.6. Let f and g be monotone symmetric norms (the inner and outer norms, respectively). For a clustering with cluster distance vectors \mathbf{c}_1,\dots,\mathbf{c}_k, the (f,g)-cost is

f\big(g(\mathbf{c}_1),\dots,g(\mathbf{c}_k)\big).

This framework subsumes k-center, k-median, Min-Sum of Radii, and Min-Load k-Clustering.

Known bounds (uncapacitated, polynomial time). Herold, Kipouridis & Spoerhase (SODA 2025) give:

· O(\log^2 n) for (\mathsf{Sym},L_1),
· O(k) for (\mathsf{Sym},\mathsf{Sym}),
· \widetilde{O}(\min\{n^{\chi_f},k^{1-\chi_g}\}) for general f,g, where \chi_h\in[0,1] is the attenuation of norm h.

Caveat for the FPT extension. Even in the uncapacitated polynomial-time setting, general (f,g)-clustering has only an O(k)-approximation. An FPT (3+\varepsilon)-approximation for capacitated (f,g)-clustering would therefore improve the polynomial-time state of the art, not merely adapt it. This is a strong caveat: the extension is not a routine consequence of the VectorClust framework.

2.10 Assumptions

We assume the metric space is finite and distances can be computed or approximated efficiently. For the theoretical algorithm, we work with integer distances after scaling and rounding (the standard technique for clustering approximations). We also assume capacities are integral and satisfy \sum_i u_i\ge n (otherwise the instance is infeasible). For the multi-resource case, we require that the total demand for each resource does not exceed the total capacity.

All results are purely mathematical. No experiments, physical tests, or code training were performed.


Chapter 3: Mathematical Background

3.1 Monotone Symmetric Norms

Let \mathbb{R}^n_{\ge 0} be the non-negative orthant. A function f : \mathbb{R}^n_{\ge 0} \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} is a monotone symmetric norm if it satisfies:

1. Positive definiteness. f(\mathbf{x}) = 0 \iff \mathbf{x} = \mathbf{0}.
2. Homogeneity. f(\lambda \mathbf{x}) = \lambda f(\mathbf{x}) for all \lambda \ge 0.
3. Subadditivity. f(\mathbf{x} + \mathbf{y}) \le f(\mathbf{x}) + f(\mathbf{y}).
4. Monotonicity. \mathbf{x} \le \mathbf{y} coordinatewise \implies f(\mathbf{x}) \le f(\mathbf{y}).
5. Symmetry. f(\mathbf{x}) = f(\pi(\mathbf{x})) for every coordinate permutation \pi.

The class of monotone symmetric norms is exactly the class of symmetric gauge functions; it includes L_p norms, top-\ell norms, and ordered weighted norms.

3.2 Representation via Top-\ell Norms

Definition 3.1 (Top-\ell norm). For \ell \in \{0,1,\dots,n\} and \mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^n_{\ge 0},

\|\mathbf{x}\|_{\text{top-}\ell} := \min_{t \ge 0}\left(\sum_{j=1}^{n} (x_j - t)_+ + \ell t\right),

where (z)_+ = \max\{0,z\}. The optimum is attained at t = x_{(\ell)}, the \ell-th largest coordinate.

Lemma 3.2 (Representation). For every monotone symmetric norm f, there exist weights w_1 \ge w_2 \ge \dots \ge w_n \ge 0 with w_{n+1} := 0 such that for all \mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^n_{\ge 0},

f(\mathbf{x}) = \sum_{\ell=1}^{n} (w_\ell - w_{\ell+1}) \|\mathbf{x}\|_{\text{top-}\ell}.

Conversely, every non-negative linear combination of top-\ell norms is a monotone symmetric norm.

Proof. The top-\ell norms generate the extreme rays of the cone of monotone symmetric norms. This is a standard result in the theory of symmetric norms; a complete proof appears in Chakrabarty & Swamy (2019), Simultaneously Approximating All Norms, and is used in the same form by Herold, Kipouridis & Spoerhase (SODA 2025) and Dai, Li & Peng (ICALP 2026). \square

Consequence. Approximating an arbitrary monotone symmetric norm reduces to approximating every top-\ell norm simultaneously. This is the structural basis of VectorClust.

3.3 Fixed-Parameter Tractability

A problem parameterized by k is fixed-parameter tractable (FPT) if it admits an algorithm of the form g(k) \cdot \operatorname{poly}(n). For clustering, k is the number of open facilities (GPUs). In vector search, k is typically small, making FPT algorithms practically relevant even when g(k) is exponential.

3.4 Hardness Landscape for Capacitated Clustering

The following lower bounds are established in the literature and directly constrain what VectorClust can claim.

Theorem 3.3 (Hardness).

1. Capacitated k-center (L_\infty). There is no (3-\varepsilon)-approximation unless P = NP. Under \mathrm{FPT} \neq W[1], there is no FPT algorithm achieving 3-\varepsilon.
2. Capacitated k-median (L_1). The best known FPT upper bound is 3+\varepsilon (Cohen-Addad & Li, ICALP 2019; recovered by Dai et al.). The best known lower bound for the capacitated version is inherited from the uncapacitated case: 1+2/e-\varepsilon assuming Gap-ETH. The tight FPT factor for capacitated k-median is open — it lies in [1+2/e, 3].
3. Uncapacitated k-median. The tight FPT factor is 1+2/e+\varepsilon (Cohen-Addad et al., ICALP 2019), matching the Gap-ETH lower bound.
4. Top-\ell norms. Dai, Li & Peng (ICALP 2026) give a tight (1 + 2/(ec) + \varepsilon)-approximation for the uncapacitated top-cn norm. For the capacitated case with the general norm, their bound is (3+\varepsilon).

Important correction. An earlier version of this thesis claimed that the factor 3+O(\varepsilon) is optimal for L_1. That claim is false for the uncapacitated case and unproven for the capacitated case. The corrected statement is given in Theorem 5.4 below.

3.5 Coresets and Sensitivity Sampling

Definition 3.4 (Coreset). Let P be a point set and \mathcal{Q} a family of queries. A weighted subset (S, w) with S \subseteq P is an \varepsilon-coreset if for every q \in \mathcal{Q},

\left|\sum_{s \in S} w(s) f(s,q) - \sum_{p \in P} f(p,q)\right| \le \varepsilon \sum_{p \in P} f(p,q).

Theorem 3.5 (Sensitivity sampling; Braverman et al., 2021). If the total sensitivity of a point set under a cost function is t, then sampling O(t \log t / \varepsilon^2) points with probability proportional to sensitivity yields an \varepsilon-coreset with constant probability.

Theorem 3.6 (Capacitated k-median coreset; Cohen-Addad & Li, ICALP 2019). There is a coreset of size O(k^2 \log^2 n / \varepsilon^3) for capacitated k-median in general metric spaces. This construction is unconditional.

Open problem. Coresets for general monotone symmetric norms under capacity constraints are not established in the literature. The reduction via Lemma 3.2 (decomposing f into top-\ell components) does not immediately preserve sensitivity bounds under capacity constraints; each top-\ell component may require separate sampling, and the union bound over n components can blow up the coreset size unless a tighter argument is found.

3.6 (f,g)-Clustering

Definition 3.7. Let f and g be monotone symmetric norms (the inner and outer norms, respectively). For a clustering with cluster distance vectors \mathbf{c}_1, \dots, \mathbf{c}_k, the (f,g)-cost is

f\big(g(\mathbf{c}_1), \dots, g(\mathbf{c}_k)\big).

This framework subsumes k-center, k-median, Min-Sum of Radii, and Min-Load k-Clustering.

Known bounds (uncapacitated, polynomial time). Herold et al. (SODA 2025) give:

· O(\log^2 n) for (\mathsf{Sym}, L_1),
· O(k) for (\mathsf{Sym}, \mathsf{Sym}),
· \widetilde{O}(\min\{n^{\chi_f}, k^{1-\chi_g}\}) for general f, g, where \chi_h \in [0,1] is the attenuation of norm h.

Caveat for the FPT extension. Even in the uncapacitated polynomial-time setting, general (f,g)-clustering has only an O(k)-approximation. An FPT (3+\varepsilon)-approximation for capacitated (f,g)-clustering would therefore improve the polynomial-time state of the art, not merely adapt it. This is a strong caveat: the extension is not a routine consequence of the VectorClust framework.

