Chapter 5: Approximation Analysis

5.1 The Threshold Lemma

Lemma 5.1 (Threshold bound). For every t \ge 0,

\sum_{j\in V}\big(d^{\mathrm{ALG}}_j - (2+O(\varepsilon))t\big)_+
\le (2+O(\varepsilon))\sum_{j\in V}(d^*_j - t)_+.

Status: two gaps.

Gap A (close-client radius). The proof considers “close” clients with d^*_j \le t and bounds the cluster radius r_C by (1+\varepsilon)t. But r_C is the maximum distance over the entire cluster, including far clients with d^*_j > t. If any far client exists, r_C can be much larger than t, invalidating the bound. A corrected proof must partition the cluster into its close and far components and assign radii per component, or use a per-client radius derived from the guessing phase.

Gap B (far-client excess). The bound

|C_{>t}| \cdot r_C \le (2+O(\varepsilon)) \sum_{j\in C}(d^*_j - t)_+

is asserted without proof. A correct argument must show that the number of far clients times the cluster radius is controlled by the excess of the far clients over t. This is plausible when r_C \le (1+\varepsilon)\max_j d^*_j and t is at most a constant fraction of the maximum, but the constant must be tracked explicitly.

Consequence. Lemma 5.1 should be presented as conditional. The unweighted, unconstrained-outlier version is proved in Dai, Li & Peng (ICALP 2026, Lemma 5.2). Transferring it to the capacitated vector-search setting requires the two fixes above.

5.2 Top-\ell Bound

Theorem 5.2 (Top-\ell approximation, conditional on Lemma 5.1). For every \ell \in \{1,\dots,n\},

\|\mathbf{d}^{\mathrm{ALG}}\|_{\text{top-}\ell} \le (3+O(\varepsilon))\|\mathbf{d}^*\|_{\text{top-}\ell}.

Proof. Let t^* = d^*_{(\ell)}. By Definition 3.1,

\|\mathbf{d}^*\|_{\text{top-}\ell} = \sum_j (d^*_j - t^*)_+ + \ell t^*.

Set t = (1+\varepsilon)t^*. Apply Lemma 5.1:

\sum_j (d^{\mathrm{ALG}}_j - (2+O(\varepsilon))t)_+ \le (2+O(\varepsilon))\sum_j (d^*_j - t)_+.

Using the variational form of the top-\ell norm with threshold (2+O(\varepsilon))t,

\|\mathbf{d}^{\mathrm{ALG}}\|_{\text{top-}\ell}
\le (2+O(\varepsilon))\sum_j (d^*_j - t^*)_+ + (2+O(\varepsilon))(1+\varepsilon)\ell t^*.

Since (1+\varepsilon) = 1+O(\varepsilon) and \ell t^* \le \|\mathbf{d}^*\|_{\text{top-}\ell},

\|\mathbf{d}^{\mathrm{ALG}}\|_{\text{top-}\ell} \le (2+O(\varepsilon))\|\mathbf{d}^*\|_{\text{top-}\ell} + O(\varepsilon)\|\mathbf{d}^*\|_{\text{top-}\ell}
= (3+O(\varepsilon))\|\mathbf{d}^*\|_{\text{top-}\ell}.

\square

5.3 General Monotone Symmetric Norm

Theorem 5.3 (Main approximation, conditional on Lemma 5.1). For every monotone symmetric norm f,

f(\mathbf{d}^{\mathrm{ALG}}) \le (3+O(\varepsilon))\,f(\mathbf{d}^*).

Proof. By Lemma 3.2, write f = \sum_\ell c_\ell \|\cdot\|_{\text{top-}\ell} with c_\ell \ge 0. Apply Theorem 5.2 to each term and sum. \square

5.4 Corrected Optimality Statement

Theorem 5.4 (Corrected).

(i) For f = L_\infty (capacitated k-center), the factor 3+O(\varepsilon) is optimal under \mathrm{FPT} \neq W[1]. This lower bound holds even for the uncapacitated k-supplier problem, making the optimality claim stronger.

(ii) For f = L_1 (capacitated k-median), the tight FPT factor is unknown. VectorClust achieves the best known upper bound 3+O(\varepsilon). The best known lower bound is 1+2/e-\varepsilon (inherited from the uncapacitated case under Gap-ETH). We claim neither optimality nor non-optimality.

(iii) For a general monotone symmetric norm f, we conjecture that 3+O(\varepsilon) is optimal, but no proof or lower bound is known.

Discussion. The correction in (ii) is essential. The (1+2/e+\varepsilon) result of Cohen-Addad et al. (ICALP 2019) applies to uncapacitated k-median. For the capacitated version, the best known FPT upper bound is 3+\varepsilon, due to Cohen-Addad & Li (ICALP 2019) and recovered by Dai et al. Cohen-Addad & Li themselves state that the best known lower bound for the capacitated version “is not higher than the 1+2/e lower bound for the uncapacitated version.” Tightening the gap [1+2/e, 3] is Open Problem 1.

5.5 Bi-Criteria Result

Dai, Li & Peng also obtain a (3, \min\{3, 1+2/e\}+\varepsilon)-bi-criteria FPT approximation for the joint (k-center, k-median) objective. This is arguably the most hardware-faithful objective for vector search, since it simultaneously controls worst-case and average latency. We include it as a benchmark for future work.

Chapter 8: Open Problems (Revised)

OP1. Tight FPT factor for capacitated k-median. Close the gap [1+2/e, 3]. Does the tight uncapacitated algorithm of Cohen-Addad et al. survive heterogeneous capacities? The capacitated setting introduces a hard combinatorial constraint that the uncapacitated algorithm does not face.

OP2. Threshold lemma. Provide a complete proof of Lemma 5.1 in the capacitated setting, closing Gaps A and B.

OP3. Rounding with per-client guarantees. Close Gap 1: prove or disprove that the capacity-correction step preserves per-client distances up to (1+\varepsilon).

OP4. Sampling coverage. Close Gap 2: prove that the LP can be perturbed so that d^{\mathrm{av}}_j \le (1+\varepsilon)d^*_j, or design a sampling scheme that does not require this inequality.

OP5. Multi-resource capacities. Even R = 2 inherits bin-packing hardness. An FPT result must parameterize by R in addition to k, or impose structure on the capacity vectors.

OP6. Symmetric weighted norms. The original “weighted top-\ell norm” is not symmetric. Two viable reformulations: (a) duplicate clients with multiplicity proportional to weight, preserving symmetry; (b) develop a new theory of weighted symmetric norms. The interaction with the threshold grid must be re-derived.

OP7. Coresets for capacitated norm objectives. Cohen-Addad & Li (ICALP 2019) give an unconditional coreset for capacitated k-median of size O(k^2 \log^2 n/\varepsilon^3). Extending this to general monotone symmetric norms under capacity is open. The top-\ell decomposition does not immediately preserve sensitivity bounds.

OP8. Capacitated (f,g)-clustering. Even uncapacitated (f,g)-clustering has only an O(k)-approximation in polynomial time. An FPT (3+\varepsilon)-approximation would improve the polynomial-time state of the art, not merely adapt it. This is the strongest caveat in the extension list.

OP9. Streaming and dynamic settings. Nothing is known even for capacitated k-median. The recourse-vs-approximation trade-off is open.

OP10. Norm-specific lower bounds. Prove or disprove that 3 is optimal for the entire monotone symmetric norm class. Dai et al.'s uncapacitated (1+2/(ce)+\varepsilon) bound for top-cn is the benchmark: as c \to 1, it approaches 1+2/e, interpolating between 3 (for c \le 1/e) and 1+2/e (for c \to 1).

OP11. Optimal outliers. Does the outlier budget force a gap above 1+2/e with capacities? The known bound is 3+\varepsilon; the optimal factor is open.

OP12. Bi-criteria latency. The capacitated analogue of Dai et al.'s FPT (3, 1+2/e+\varepsilon) bi-criteria for (k-center, k-median) is open and arguably the most hardware-faithful objective.

