Appendix A: Proof of Lemma 2.1 (Representation of Monotone Symmetric Norms)

Lemma A.1. For any monotone symmetric norm f: \mathbb{R}^n_{\ge 0} \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}, there exist weights w_1 \ge w_2 \ge \dots \ge w_n \ge 0 with w_{n+1} := 0 such that for all \mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^n_{\ge 0},

f(\mathbf{x}) = \sum_{\ell=1}^{n} (w_\ell - w_{\ell+1}) \|\mathbf{x}\|_{\text{top-}\ell}.

Conversely, every non-negative linear combination of top-\ell norms is a monotone symmetric norm.

Proof. The proof follows the standard duality theory of symmetric norms. Let \mathbf{x}^\downarrow = (x_{(1)}, \dots, x_{(n)}) denote the non-increasing rearrangement of \mathbf{x}. Since f is symmetric, it depends only on \mathbf{x}^\downarrow. Since f is monotone, it is a non-decreasing function of each coordinate of \mathbf{x}^\downarrow.

Consider the Lorenz curve of \mathbf{x}, defined by

L_{\mathbf{x}}(t) := \sum_{\ell=1}^{\lfloor t \rfloor} x_{(\ell)} + (t - \lfloor t \rfloor) x_{(\lceil t \rceil)}, \quad t \in [0, n].

The top-\ell norm is the right derivative of L_{\mathbf{x}} at \ell:

\|\mathbf{x}\|_{\text{top-}\ell} = L_{\mathbf{x}}(\ell) - L_{\mathbf{x}}(\ell-1) = \int_{\ell-1}^{\ell} dL_{\mathbf{x}}(t).

Since f is a monotone symmetric norm, it can be written as a Choquet integral with respect to a capacity w:

f(\mathbf{x}) = \int_0^\infty w(\{j : x_j > t\}) \, dt.

Because f is symmetric, the capacity w depends only on the cardinality of the set, so w(S) = w_{|S|} for some non-decreasing sequence 0 \le w_1 \le w_2 \le \dots \le w_n. Rewriting the Choquet integral in terms of the order statistics,

f(\mathbf{x}) = \sum_{\ell=1}^{n} (w_\ell - w_{\ell-1}) x_{(\ell)},

where w_0 := 0. The coefficient of x_{(\ell)} is the marginal capacity w_\ell - w_{\ell-1}. Since the top-\ell norm can be written as

\|\mathbf{x}\|_{\text{top-}\ell} = \sum_{j=1}^{\ell} x_{(j)},

we can express f(\mathbf{x}) as a linear combination of top-\ell norms:

f(\mathbf{x}) = \sum_{\ell=1}^{n} (w_\ell - w_{\ell+1}) \|\mathbf{x}\|_{\text{top-}\ell}.

Indeed, the coefficient of x_{(j)} on the right-hand side is \sum_{\ell=j}^{n} (w_\ell - w_{\ell+1}) = w_j, which matches the Choquet representation. The weights w_1 \ge w_2 \ge \dots \ge w_n \ge 0 are non-negative because w is non-decreasing. \square

Remark. This representation is standard in the theory of symmetric norms and appears in Chakrabarty & Swamy (2019) and in the framework of Dai, Li & Peng (ICALP 2026, Lemma 2.1). The key insight is that top-\ell norms are the extreme rays of the cone of monotone symmetric norms.

