Appendix C: Proof of Lemma 4.3 (Coverage)

Lemma C.1 (Coverage, conditional). With probability at least 1 - 1/n, for every optimal cluster C^* whose contribution to the objective is significant, the set R contains at least one vector from C^*, provided d^{\mathrm{av}}_j \le d^*_j for every client j.

Proof. Fix a threshold t from the geometric grid \{0, (1+\varepsilon), (1+\varepsilon)^2, \dots, \Delta\} and an optimal cluster C^*. Let

\delta := \sum_{j \in C^*} (d^*_j - t)_+.

We assume \delta is non-negligible (otherwise C^* does not contribute significantly to the objective and can be ignored).

The sampling probability for a vector j \in C^* in the second sampling step is proportional to

p_j := \max(0, (1-\varepsilon) d^{\mathrm{av}}_j - t).

Step 1: Lower bound on p_j. Under the assumption d^{\mathrm{av}}_j \le d^*_j,

p_j \ge (1-\varepsilon) \max(0, d^*_j - t) - O(\varepsilon).

Summing over j \in C^*, the total probability mass assigned to C^* is at least

\sum_{j \in C^*} p_j \ge (1-\varepsilon) \delta - O(\varepsilon) |C^*|.

Step 2: Expected number of sampled points. The algorithm samples M = O(k \log n / \varepsilon^2) points in the second step. The expected number of points from C^* is at least

\mathbb{E}[\text{points from } C^*] \ge \frac{M \cdot \sum_{j \in C^*} p_j}{\sum_{j \in V} p_j} \ge \frac{M \cdot ((1-\varepsilon) \delta - O(\varepsilon) |C^*|)}{\sum_{j \in V} p_j}.

Since \sum_{j \in V} p_j \le \sum_{j \in V} d^{\mathrm{av}}_j \le \operatorname{poly}(n), and choosing the constant in M appropriately, we can ensure

\mathbb{E}[\text{points from } C^*] \ge c \log n

for some constant c > 0 when \delta is not too small.

Step 3: Chernoff bound. By the Chernoff bound, the probability that no point from C^* is sampled is at most

\Pr[\text{no point from } C^*] \le e^{-c \log n} = n^{-c}.

Taking a union bound over all O(k \log n) clusters and O(\log n/\varepsilon) thresholds yields a total failure probability of at most n^{-c'} for a suitable constant c' > 0. Choosing constants appropriately gives failure probability 1 - 1/n.

Conclusion. Under the assumption d^{\mathrm{av}}_j \le d^*_j, the coverage guarantee holds. \square

Gap 2 (flagged). The proof requires d^{\mathrm{av}}_j \le d^*_j. This is not guaranteed by the LP: the fractional solution may route a client through a farther facility to satisfy capacity constraints. Without this inequality, the sampling weights can under-represent clusters. A corrected proof must either:

· (a) Sample with respect to a capped proxy for d^*_j computed from the LP dual, or
· (b) Prove that the LP can be perturbed to satisfy d^{\mathrm{av}}_j \le (1+\varepsilon) d^*_j, or
· (c) Use a different sampling scheme (e.g., adaptive sampling as in Abbasi et al., FOCS 2023) that does not require this inequality.

This gap is the subject of Open Problem 4.

