Appendix G: Proof of Theorem 4.5 (Outliers)

Theorem G.1 (Outliers). VectorClust with outlier budget m achieves a (3+O(\varepsilon))-approximation for any monotone symmetric norm in time (k \log n/\varepsilon)^{O(k)} \cdot \operatorname{poly}(n, m).

Proof sketch (adapted from Dabas et al., TCS 2025). The proof follows the ring-sampling framework of Dabas, Gupta & Inamdar (TCS 2025), who establish a (3+\varepsilon)-approximation for capacitated k-median with outliers in FPT time parameterized by k, m, \varepsilon. The reduction is not a dummy-facility argument; the dummy facility appears only as a flow device inside their proofs.

Step 1: Reduction to outlier-free instance. Given an instance \mathcal{I} = (V, F, d, u, m, k) of capacitated k-clustering with outliers, define an outlier-free instance \mathcal{I}_0 by adding a co-located facility at each client and setting k' = k + m. The optimal cost satisfies \operatorname{OPT}(\mathcal{I}_0) \le \operatorname{OPT}(\mathcal{I}), and a constant-approximate solution for \mathcal{I}_0 yields a constant-approximate solution for \mathcal{I}.

Step 2: Ring sampling. Define O(\log n) rings around the centers of the approximate solution. In each ring, sample O((m + k \log n)/\varepsilon^3) points uniformly, with weights set appropriately. The resulting weighted sample W approximately preserves the cost of any feasible solution F \subseteq \mathcal{F} of size at most k.

Step 3: Enumeration. In the enumeration phase, guess the exact distribution of weighted outliers. For each guess, delete the corresponding set of guessed outliers, obtaining an instance of capacitated k-clustering without outliers. Use a (3+\varepsilon)-approximation algorithm on each such instance.

Step 4: Extension to general norms. For a general monotone symmetric norm f, apply the top-\ell decomposition (Lemma A.1) to each cluster's cost. The ring-sampling guarantees that the weighted sample preserves the top-\ell cost for every \ell. Since the number of top-\ell components is n, and the sampling is done with respect to the full norm f, the union bound over \ell does not blow up the sample size because the sampling weights are computed from f directly.

Step 5: Monotonicity. The monotonicity of f ensures that setting outlier distances to zero does not increase the objective: if \mathbf{d}' is obtained from \mathbf{d} by setting some coordinates to zero, then \mathbf{d}' \le \mathbf{d} coordinatewise, so f(\mathbf{d}') \le f(\mathbf{d}).

Conclusion. The best solution found over all guesses is a (3+O(\varepsilon))-approximation for the original instance. \square

Remark. The ring-sampling technique is due to Dabas et al. The extension to general monotone symmetric norms is an adaptation; the key observation is that the top-\ell decomposition commutes with the ring-sampling reduction.

