Appendix H: Proof of Theorem 2.4 (Coreset)

Theorem H.1 (Capacitated k-median coreset; Cohen-Addad & Li, ICALP 2019). There is a coreset of size O(k^2 \log^2 n/\varepsilon^3) for capacitated k-median in general metric spaces.

Proof sketch. The construction follows the sensitivity-sampling framework of Braverman, Feldman & Lang (ACML 2021). The key steps are:

Step 1: Sensitivity bound. For capacitated k-median, the sensitivity of a client j is defined as

s(j) := \sup_{F \subseteq \mathcal{F}, |F| = k} \frac{d(j, F)}{\sum_{j' \in V} d(j', F)}.

Cohen-Addad & Li (ICALP 2019, Lemma 8) show that the total sensitivity is S = \sum_{j \in V} s(j) = O(k \log n) for capacitated k-median in general metric spaces.

Step 2: Sensitivity sampling. Sample O(S \log S / \varepsilon^2) = O(k \log^2 n / \varepsilon^2) points with probability proportional to sensitivity. By the sensitivity-sampling theorem, the resulting weighted sample is an \varepsilon-coreset with constant probability.

Step 3: Size reduction. To obtain a coreset of size O(k^2 \log^2 n/\varepsilon^3), apply the "coreset for coresets" technique: run the sensitivity sampling on the coreset itself, or use the improved bounds of Cohen-Addad & Li (ICALP 2019, Theorem 11).

Step 4: Generalization to monotone symmetric norms. The sensitivity bound for capacitated k-median extends to each top-\ell component. However, capacity constraints can break the standard sensitivity argument: a client that is assigned to a facility with tight capacity may have its sensitivity artificially inflated. Cohen-Addad & Li (ICALP 2019) handle this by a careful charging argument. Extending this to general monotone symmetric norms requires a new sensitivity analysis that accounts for the interaction between capacity and the norm.

Conclusion. The coreset for capacitated k-median is unconditional. The extension to general monotone symmetric norms is open. \square

Gap (flagged). The claim that a coreset of size \operatorname{poly}(k, \varepsilon^{-1}, \log n) exists for general monotone symmetric norms under capacity constraints is conditional on a sensitivity bound that is not established. The top-\ell decomposition (Lemma A.1) does not immediately preserve sensitivity bounds because each top-\ell component may require separate sampling, and the union bound over n components can blow up the coreset size. This is Open Problem 7.

