Appendix I: Proof of Theorem 6.5 (Federated Routing)

Theorem I.1 (Cross-partition query, conditional). If each leaf index returns an \alpha-approximate nearest neighbor and cluster diameters are bounded by c \cdot r where r is the query-to-nearest distance, then hierarchical routing returns an \alpha(1+O(\log k))-approximate nearest neighbor in O(\log k) query time.

Proof. We assume a balanced binary tree of depth d = \lceil \log_2 k \rceil. Each node v corresponds to a subset P_v of the dataset. The index at node v supports \alpha-approximate nearest neighbor queries: given a query point q, it returns a point p \in P_v such that

d(q, p) \le \alpha \cdot \min_{p' \in P_v} d(q, p').

The query procedure starts at the root. At each node v, query its index to obtain a candidate p_v \in P_v. Then recursively query the two children v_L, v_R and obtain candidates p_{v_L}, p_{v_R}. Return the best among p_v, p_{v_L}, p_{v_R}.

We prove by induction on the height h that the distance of the candidate returned from node v is at most

\alpha \cdot (1 + \beta h) \cdot \min_{p' \in P_v} d(q, p'),

where \beta is a constant depending on the cluster diameters.

Base case (h = 0). At a leaf, the index returns an \alpha-approximate nearest neighbor, so the statement holds with \beta = 0.

Inductive step. Assume the statement holds for children at height h-1. Let r = \min_{p' \in P_v} d(q, p') be the true nearest-neighbor distance in P_v. Suppose without loss of generality that the nearest neighbor lies in v_L. Then \min_{p' \in P_{v_L}} d(q, p') \le r. By the induction hypothesis, the candidate from v_L has distance at most \alpha(1 + \beta(h-1)) r.

The candidate from the index at v is at most \alpha r_v, where r_v is the distance from q to the cluster center of the cluster containing the nearest neighbor. Condition (diameter bound): Since the cluster diameter is at most c r for some constant c, we have r_v \le r + c r = (1+c)r. Thus, the candidate from the index at v has distance at most \alpha(1+c)r.

Taking the best among the three candidates, the distance is at most

\alpha \cdot \max((1+c)r, (1+\beta(h-1))r).

Choose \beta such that 1 + \beta h \ge \max(1+c, 1+\beta(h-1)). For example, take \beta = c. Then the bound becomes \alpha(1 + c h) r. Since h \le \lceil \log_2 k \rceil, we have 1 + c \log_2 k = O(\log k).

The query time is O(\log k) times the per-node index query time, because we visit O(1) nodes per level. \square

Caveat. The diameter bound c \cdot r is not implied by capacitated clustering alone. It holds, for example, under bounded doubling dimension. Without this condition, the proof fails. This is stated as a hypothesis in Theorem 6.5.

